room      prop_1
kind      geometry
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section II, Proposition I; Plate 2, Fig. 5.
caption   The areas which revolving bodies describe by radii drawn to an immoveable centre of force are proportional to the times in which they are described.
final     4
ceiling   eq0 :: \Delta t_1 = \Delta t_2 = \cdots \quad\Rightarrow\quad AB,\,BC,\,CD,\ldots \text{ (inertial steps, Law I)}
ceiling   eq1 :: A_{\triangle}(SAB) = A_{\triangle}(SBC) = A_{\triangle}(SCD) = \cdots
ceiling   eq2 :: Bc = AB,\ \ Cc \parallel SB \;\Rightarrow\; [SBC] = [SBc] = [SAB]
ceiling   eq3 :: \lim_{\Delta t \to 0}\ \text{polygon} \to \text{curve},\quad \dfrac{dA}{dt} = \text{const}\;(\text{areas} \propto \text{times})

station 1
  gloss   The centre of force S is fixed; the body flies in straight polygonal jumps ABCDEF, one per equal time-slice.
  color   centerorange #E8770A
  color   pathblue     #1E6FE0
  panel
    point    S  @(0.5,4.8)  marker=dot label=$S$ at=NW color=centerorange
    point    A  @(1.0,0.4)  marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    B  @(3.2,0.9)  marker=dot label=$B$ at=S
    point    C  @(5.0,2.2)  marker=dot label=$C$ at=SE
    point    D  @(5.8,4.0)  marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    point    E  @(5.4,5.8)  marker=dot label=$E$ at=NE
    point    F  @(4.2,7.0)  marker=dot label=$F$ at=N
    polyline path A B C D E F color=pathblue heart label=$ABCDEF$ at=SE
  text
    Fix the {centerorange|centre of force $S$} — an immoveable point that pulls on the body. \
    Now chop the flow of time into equal tiny slices $\Delta t$, and watch the body hop along the \
    {pathblue|polygonal path $ABCDEF$}: in each slice it flies dead-straight by pure inertia (\textbf{Law I}), \
    then receives one sudden centripetal thump toward $S$ at each vertex. This is Newton's \textit{impulse \
    approximation} — the method of first and last ratios (\textbf{Lemma V, Cor.~4}) applied not to figures but to \
    \emph{motion}. If we can show every little triangle $S$ sweeps out has the same area, we will have proved \
    that area swept is proportional to elapsed time — Kepler's Second Law, freed from ellipses and made true for \
    \emph{any} central force whatsoever.

station 2
  gloss   The radii SA,SB,SC,SD,SE,SF are the sweeping lines that trace out the swept triangles.
  color   pathblue  #1E6FE0
  color   radigreen #00A35A
  color   centerorange #E8770A
  panel
    point    S  @(0.5,4.8)  marker=dot label=$S$ at=NW color=centerorange
    point    A  @(1.0,0.4)  marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    B  @(3.2,0.9)  marker=dot label=$B$ at=S
    point    C  @(5.0,2.2)  marker=dot label=$C$ at=SE
    point    D  @(5.8,4.0)  marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    point    E  @(5.4,5.8)  marker=dot label=$E$ at=NE
    point    F  @(4.2,7.0)  marker=dot label=$F$ at=N
    polyline path A B C D E F color=pathblue label=$ABCDEF$ at=SE
    segment  SA  S A color=radigreen heart label=$SA$ at=W
    segment  SB  S B color=radigreen heart label=$SB$ at=S
    segment  SC  S C color=radigreen heart label=$SC$ at=E
    segment  SD  S D color=radigreen heart label=$SD$ at=E
    segment  SE  S E color=radigreen heart label=$SE$ at=E
    segment  SF  S F color=radigreen heart label=$SF$ at=E
  text
    Draw the {radigreen|radii $SA,\,SB,\,SC,\,SD,\,SE,\,SF$} from the centre to every vertex of the path. \
    As the body advances one step, its radius sweeps across a thin triangle: the area cut out between \
    successive radii is precisely the area described "by a radius drawn to the centre." Kepler saw this pattern \
    in the wanderings of Mars — the planet moves fast near the Sun and slow when far, yet always paints equal \
    areas in equal times. Newton's aim is to \emph{explain} it: he will prove that triangle $SAB$, then $SBC$, \
    then $SCD$, and so on are all exactly equal, so that the running total of {radigreen|swept area} climbs in \
    perfect lockstep with the clock. In modern language this equal-area law is nothing but the conservation of \
    angular momentum, hiding in plain geometric sight.

station 3
  gloss   The three key triangles SAB, SBc, SBC are equal — the heart of the impulse argument.
  color   radigreen    #00A35A
  color   arearpurple  #8E24AA
  color   centerorange #E8770A
  panel
    point    S  @(0.5,4.8)  marker=dot label=$S$ at=NW color=centerorange
    point    A  @(1.0,0.4)  marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    B  @(3.2,0.9)  marker=dot label=$B$ at=S
    point    C  @(5.0,2.2)  marker=dot label=$C$ at=SE
    point    c  @(5.4,1.4)  marker=dot label=$c$ at=SE
    segment  Bc  B c color=arearpurple label=$Bc$ at=S
    segment  cC  c C color=arearpurple label=$Cc$ at=E
    segment  SB  S B color=radigreen label=$SB$ at=W
    polygon  SAB S A B color=arearpurple heart label=$SAB$ at=W
    polygon  SBc S B c color=arearpurple heart label=$SBc$ at=SE
    polygon  SBC S B C color=arearpurple heart label=$SBC$ at=E
  text
    Here is the whole proof in miniature. Had no force acted at $B$, inertia (\textbf{Law I}) would carry the \
    body straight on to $c$, with {arearpurple|$Bc = AB$}; then triangles $SAB$ and $SBc$ share the base \
    {radigreen|$SB$} and have equal heights, so \emph{$SAB = SBc$}. But at $B$ a single centripetal impulse toward \
    $S$ deflects the body, and by the parallelogram of motions (\textbf{Law II, Cor.~1}) it lands at $C$ where \
    {arearpurple|$Cc \parallel SB$}. Because $Cc$ is parallel to $SB$, triangles $SBc$ and $SBC$ stand on the same \
    base $SB$ between the same parallels — so \emph{$SBc = SBC$}, and therefore \emph{$SBC = SAB$}. This is \
    Newton's genius: an impulse pointed straight at $S$ bends the body's \emph{direction} but leaves the swept \
    {arearpurple|triangular area} untouched.

station 4
  gloss   The impulse Cc is always parallel to the radius; in the limit the polygon becomes a smooth curve and areas ∝ times.
  color   pathblue     #1E6FE0
  color   radigreen    #00A35A
  color   impulsered   #D81B60
  color   centerorange #E8770A
  panel
    point    S  @(0.5,4.8)  marker=dot label=$S$ at=NW color=centerorange
    point    A  @(1.0,0.4)  marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    B  @(3.2,0.9)  marker=dot label=$B$ at=S
    point    C  @(5.0,2.2)  marker=dot label=$C$ at=SE
    point    D  @(5.8,4.0)  marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    point    E  @(5.4,5.8)  marker=dot label=$E$ at=NE
    point    F  @(4.2,7.0)  marker=dot label=$F$ at=N
    point    c  @(5.4,1.4)  marker=dot label=$c$ at=SE
    polyline path A B C D E F color=pathblue heart label=$ABCDEF$ at=NW
    segment  Bc  B c color=pathblue label=$Bc$ at=S
    segment  SB  S B color=radigreen label=$SB$ at=W
    ray      imp c C color=impulsered heart label=$Cc \parallel SB$ at=SE
  text
    Repeat the same argument at $C$, $D$, $E$, and on: each {impulsered|centripetal impulse $Cc$, always parallel \
    to the radius $SB$}, preserves the swept area, so $SCD = SBC$, $SDE = SCD$, and every triangle equals every \
    other. By composition the whole {pathblue|swept region $SADFS$} grows exactly as the time elapses. Now let the \
    number of triangles increase without bound and their breadth shrink to nothing: by \textbf{Lemma V, Cor.~4} the \
    ultimate perimeter $ADF$ becomes a smooth {pathblue|curve}, the pulsing thumps merge into a force acting \
    \emph{continually}, and the equal-area rule passes unbroken to the limit — $\dfrac{dA}{dt} = \text{const}$. \
    This holds for \emph{any} central force, however it varies with distance: the force sculpts the \emph{shape} of \
    the orbit, but never touches the sacred equal-area law. \textit{Q.E.D.}
